Synthesize the following 3 reviews into a meta-review:

- {"summary": "The paper presents a theoretical framework for continual self-play learning, focusing on the computational expressiveness of the training loop under resource constraints. It introduces a generator-executor-verifier-buffer loop and characterizes its expressiveness using SPACE complexity classes. The paper also discusses monotone capability growth and separates capability universality from alignment and safety.", "significance_and_novelty": "The paper provides a novel process-level characterization of self-play learning, linking it to classical space hierarchies, which is a significant theoretical contribution.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["The paper introduces a novel framework linking self-play learning to SPACE complexity classes (Section 1.2).", "It provides a detailed theoretical analysis of the expressiveness of the training loop (Section 6.1).", "The separation of capability universality from alignment and safety is conceptually valuable (Abstract)."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["The paper's practical applicability is limited due to strong assumptions like unbounded buffers and ideal exploration (Section G.1).", "The empirical evaluation is lacking, with no experiments to support theoretical claims (entire paper).", "The complexity of the theoretical framework may hinder its accessibility and practical implementation (Section G.2)."], "suggestions": ["Consider providing empirical validation to support theoretical claims.", "Clarify the practical implications and limitations of the assumptions made in the theoretical framework."], "rating": 5, "confidence": 4, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 3, "contribution": 3}

- {"summary": "The paper explores the computational power of self-play training loops in reinforcement learning, focusing on the expressiveness of the training process. It introduces a generator-executor-verifier-buffer loop framework, demonstrating Turing-completeness and a complexity hierarchy based on resource constraints. The paper highlights monotone capability growth and curriculum learnability mechanisms.", "significance_and_novelty": "The paper provides a novel framework linking self-play training loops to Turing-completeness and SPACE complexity classes, offering new insights into the expressiveness of reinforcement learning processes.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["The paper establishes a novel connection between self-play training loops and Turing-completeness, as shown in Section 6.1.", "It introduces a complexity hierarchy based on resource constraints, providing a new perspective on capability growth (Section 1.2).", "The framework offers a process-level characterization of expressiveness, separating it from architectural completeness (Abstract)."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["The focus on expressiveness over efficiency limits practical applicability, as noted in Section 1.2.", "The paper does not address catastrophic forgetting at the weight level, which is a significant limitation (Limitations section)."], "suggestions": ["Consider addressing catastrophic forgetting in future work to enhance the practical applicability of the framework."], "rating": 6, "confidence": 4, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 3, "contribution": 3}

- {"summary": "The paper investigates the computational power of a continual self-play training loop, introducing a process-level characterization of its expressiveness and a modular Coverage-Persistence-Learning (CPL) decomposition. It provides theoretical guarantees and positions self-play as a framework for data-free improvement under resource constraints.", "significance_and_novelty": "The paper offers a novel process-level characterization of self-play training loops, linking them to classical space hierarchies, which is a significant theoretical advancement.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["The paper introduces a novel process-level characterization of self-play training loops, linking them to classical space hierarchies (Section 1.2).", "It provides a modular CPL decomposition that offers a structured approach to understanding self-play dynamics (Section 1.2).", "Theoretical guarantees are provided, offering insights into the computational limits and capabilities of self-play systems (Section 1.2)."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["The empirical evaluation lacks depth, with limited discussion on practical implementation and results (Section E.2).", "The paper's clarity is hindered by complex theoretical explanations that may be difficult for practitioners to follow (Section C.4)."], "suggestions": ["Improve the clarity of theoretical sections to make them more accessible to practitioners (Section C.4).", "Expand the empirical evaluation to include more practical implementation details and results (Section E.2)."], "rating": 6, "confidence": 4, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 2, "contribution": 3}
